# Chapter 13 — Make a Game

This is the one the whole book has been walking toward. You have a screen to draw
on, a tick that beats sixty times a second, buttons in your hand, sound, motion,
collision, and a scene full of things. Every piece is on the table. This chapter
snaps them together into a small, complete, *playable* game — a hero, a coin, a
rule, and a way to win.

A game is not a big idea. It is four small ones stacked: **set up the world**,
**give it a rule**, **run the rule every tick**, and **check for a win**. Build
those four and you have a game. Everything fancier — levels, enemies, score bars,
music — is more of the same four, layered on. Let us build the smallest one that
is still a real game, and you will see the whole shape at once.

## Set the stage

First the world. Turn gravity off so nothing drifts, spawn a hero on the left and
a coin to its right, and start a score at zero. Then look at what you have:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define score 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 0 10))
(define coin (world/spawn 'coin 20 10))
(display (list score (world/count 'coin)))
(newline)
```

```
(0 1)
```

A score of `0` and `1` coin in the world — that is the opening position of your
game, printed so you can check it. Nothing is moving yet; this is the board set
up before the first move. Everything from here is the hero crossing the gap to
that coin, and you counting what happens.

## Give it a rule

A game is its *rules*. Here is the only rule this game needs: **if the hero is
touching the coin, take the coin and add a point.** Written as a function, it
reads almost like that sentence:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define score 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 0 10))
(define coin (world/spawn 'coin 20 10))
(define (collect!)
  (when (and (entity/alive? coin) (entity/overlaps? hero coin))
    (entity/despawn! coin)
    (set! score (+ score 1))))
(collect!)
(display score)
(newline)
```

```
0
```

You defined `collect!` and ran it once — and the score is still `0`. That is
correct: the hero is at `0` and the coin at `20`, so they are *not* touching, so
the rule does nothing. A rule that fires only when it should is a rule you can
trust. The hero has not moved yet, so there is nothing to collect. That is the
next piece.

## Run the rule every tick

Now the game *moves*. Give the hero a velocity so the tick from Chapter 5 carries
it rightward, and define an `update` that does one frame of the game: step the
world, then check the rule. Call `update` twice — two frames — and watch:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/gravity! 0 0)
(define score 0)
(define hero (world/spawn 'hero 0 10))
(define coin (world/spawn 'coin 20 10))
(define (collect!)
  (when (and (entity/alive? coin) (entity/overlaps? hero coin))
    (entity/despawn! coin)
    (set! score (+ score 1))))
(entity/set-vel! hero 5 0)
(define (update) (world/step) (collect!))
(update)
(update)
(display (list score (world/count 'coin)))
(newline)
```

```
(1 0)
```

**That is a game happening.** The hero moved under its own velocity, the tick
carried it into the coin, the `collect!` rule saw the overlap and fired — the
coin is gone and the score is `1`. Read the loop again: `update` steps the world
and checks the rule, every single frame. On a live console that loop runs sixty
times a second and your whole game lives inside it. Yours just ran twice and a
coin got collected. Same shape, fewer frames.

## Check for a win

A game needs an ending. This one is won when there are no coins left — so the
win condition is just a question you ask the scene from Chapter 12:

```motoi
(require 'game/world)
(world/spawn 'coin 10 10)
(world/spawn 'coin 30 10)
(define (won?) (= (world/count 'coin) 0))
(display (won?))
(newline)
```

```
#f
```

Two coins on the board, so `won?` is `#f` — not won yet. Collect them both and
`(world/count 'coin)` reaches `0` and `won?` flips to `#t`. This is the whole
end-game in one line: no separate scorekeeper to drift out of sync, just the
world counting itself. Every frame your live game would ask `(won?)` and, the
instant it turns `#t`, roll the victory screen.

## The whole game, in one breath

Step back and see what you built. **Set up** a hero and a coin. **Rule:** touch
the coin, take it, score. **Loop:** every tick, move and check. **Win:** no coins
left. Four ideas. That is not a toy version of a game — that *is* a game, the
same skeleton under every platformer, every collect-'em-up, every dodge-the-thing
arcade game ever made. Bigger games are this skeleton wearing more: ten coins
instead of one, enemies with their own rules, a `world/step` loop that also reads
your buttons and plays a sound on pickup.

And that is the real secret this book has been keeping. The console does not hand
you a game — it hands you a hero, a tick, a rule, and a count, and *you* arrange
them. The IDE and the little TUI do not have every power a big engine has. They
do not need to. With these few verbs and a rule you wrote yourself, you can make
something real, right now — and your imagination and your skill are what make the
next one better. That is what it means to make a game.

## What you just proved

You made a game. You set up a world with a hero, a coin, and a score; wrote a
rule that collects the coin on contact; ran that rule inside an update loop while
the tick carried the hero across; and watched the coin vanish and the score tick
to `1`. Then you wrote a win condition out of nothing but the world's own census.
Four small programs, and together they are a complete, playable loop — every one
ran and reported a real result you could check.

You have the whole machine now. What is left in this book is polish and pride:
giving your game a soundtrack, letting the console remember a high score between
plays, and packing the whole thing into a cart you can hand to a friend. The next
chapter picks up the synth from Chapter 8 and turns loose notes into music.

> **Tip**
>
> Build every game as these four parts, in this order: *set up*, *rule*, *loop*,
> *win*. When a game misbehaves, you almost always know which part to look at —
> nothing spawns? the setup. Nothing happens on contact? the rule. Nothing moves?
> the loop. Never ends? the win check. Keeping the four separate keeps them
> fixable.

> **Warning**
>
> At the bare prompt you drive the game by calling `update` yourself, and you fake
> the player by giving the hero a velocity — there are no buttons to read and no
> frame loop to pump them. A live console flips both on: `on-tick` runs your
> `update` sixty times a second, and `input/down?` from Chapter 6 reads the real
> player's hand instead of a velocity you set. The logic you wrote here does not
> change; the console just starts turning the crank for you.

## Cross-references

- [[book-of-motoi#chapter-5]] — the tick, the loop that runs your game's update.
- [[book-of-motoi#chapter-6]] — the buttons, the live player's hand on the game.
- [[book-of-motoi#chapter-11]] — collision, the rule at the heart of this game.
- [[book-of-motoi#chapter-12]] — the scene and `world/count`, your win detector.

---

*Turn the page. We give the game a soundtrack.*
